@meta
id: 03-05
title: Open a line above
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: open-O
par: 50
minutes: 4
vscode: click at the start of line one, type, press Enter
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
The import of `injectable` is missing, so the decorator below has nothing
to bind to. It belongs above the existing import, on the first line of the
file:

    import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

Line one is an awkward place to add something above when you think in
clicks and Enters, because there is no line above to click on. Vim has a
key for it, and it is the capital of the one you learned last lesson.

@keys
| `o` | open a line below |
| `O` | open a line above |
| `Esc` | out |
| `u` | undo, if you open one too many |

@hints
Same key as last time, but you want the new line on the other side of the
cursor. Try the capital.
--
`O` opens a line above the current one and puts you in insert mode on it.
On line one that gives you a new first line, which is exactly the problem
you have.
--
The cursor already starts on line one, so you do not need to move. `O`,
type the import, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    O import { injectable } from 'tsyringe'; <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Forty-four keystrokes, thirty-eight of them the import itself. That ratio
is the goal for the rest of your vim career: the overhead of getting into
position rounds to nothing, and almost everything you press is content.

`o` and `O` are a pair in the same way as `i`/`I` and `a`/`A`: lowercase
goes the direction you read, capital goes the other way. Adding an import
at the top of a file, a comment above a function, or a guard clause above
the code it guards — all `O`.

One habit to build now: after `O` or `o`, press `Esc` as soon as the line
is typed. Do not press Enter to "get out of the way" and leave a blank
line behind. The undo in your hands is `u`, and it costs nothing.

@start payment.service.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}
